3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74138" :question/problem "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74138/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74138"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5538"] :question/problem "Given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044632/assets/eec8345a2a1eb622aafae6890c6548efcd5b595d18334b5bad9e43f8b0fd4534.png) find $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5539"] :question/problem "Given the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044632/assets/c3261bc45f978551da6b9ae98489e4eeceadfb5a4669a703736b3d623ed2c41d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044632/assets/40f7e2f6c5aaff24c18d65776fbbb62f44effeb921bc6e54a4e4f7ada905e614.png) find the distance $\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the distance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{y}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1-2)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}+(3-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6190"] :question/problem "If $\\left\\Vert {- \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{x} }{2} } \\right\\Vert = 5,$ find $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $\\Vert x \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\frac{x}{2}‖ & =5 \\\\ ‖-\\frac{1}{2}x‖ & =5 \\\\ |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\cdot ‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3x‖ & =3\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5540"] :question/problem "Normalize the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044632/assets/33fcf438acd77616d7f5c6881234f9dcc87136575b741af6af72eee9d4401dfb.png)" :question/worked-solution "To normalize a vector $\\mathbf{x},$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|}.$ Therefore, to normalize $\\mathbf{x},$ we first calculate $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $\\mathbf{x}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8}{10} \\\\ \\frac{6}{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
